****
Compared to the other Helium lines, this one has a very strong signal. While the features are about the same as in yellow, 
the He I D3 line is nowhere near this strong. I may be the first amateur to directly image the He I 10830 Sun. The proof of concept precedent I know of is a Sun of lower resolution, a continuum subtracted image of Christian Buil -- who provided valuable advice to reach this deep. 

Affectionately, I call this region the Challenger Deep, the coincidence of the numbers on the one hand, 
and the several challenges of building and operating a spectroheliograph at this wavelength on the other. 
Visually black materials could be shiny bright at 1100nm, the CMOS sensor is out of spec and its efficiency hits rock bottom, to name just two.

This helium line readily shows the imprint of coronal holes. 
I could show them in both He I D3, and somewhat in He I 7065, with math, but this line shows them directly, on the reconstructed disk, without further math. 
Other helium lines, such as that at 4472 with prominences and filaments, 
the mostly hidden 5017, with very dim prominences and bright flares, 
the 6678 restricted to dim prominences and the light blanket above the active regions are dwarfed even by the He I D3 line, which in turn is a mere shadow of this line.
